# ── PUBLIC (bundled into client JS — never put secrets here) ──────────────
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000

NEXT_PUBLIC_PADDLE_CLIENT_TOKEN=
NEXT_PUBLIC_PADDLE_ENVIRONMENT=sandbox

# PostHog — see docs/ANALYTICS_SETUP.md. The site works normally with all
# four of these unset; analytics just stays a no-op.
NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_KEY=
NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_HOST=https://us.i.posthog.com

# ── SERVER-ONLY (never exposed to the browser) ─────────────────────────────
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

RESEND_API_KEY=
FROM_EMAIL="Not4Normal <hello@not4normal.store>"

PADDLE_API_KEY=
PADDLE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

POSTHOG_PERSONAL_API_KEY=
POSTHOG_PROJECT_ID=

# Personal Brand Content OS — AI analysis + AI Planner (src/lib/ai/).
# Server-only, plain HTTP to the Anthropic Messages API — never sent to
# the browser. The site works normally with this unset; the AI analysis
# and AI Planner features just report "not configured" instead of
# erroring. ANTHROPIC_MODEL is optional and defaults to claude-opus-5.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
ANTHROPIC_MODEL=

# Personal Brand Content OS — Instagram sync (src/lib/instagram/). Pulls
# views/likes/comments/saves/shares for posted content via the Facebook
# Graph API on-demand (the "Sync from Instagram" button in Content
# Library), never automatically. The site works normally with this
# unset; the sync button just reports "not configured" instead of
# erroring. INSTAGRAM_BUSINESS_ACCOUNT_ID is the numeric ID of the
# Instagram professional account linked to your Facebook Page — see
# docs/INSTAGRAM_SYNC_SETUP.md for how to get both values.
#
# INSTAGRAM_ACCESS_TOKEN = the Facebook Graph API token used ONLY for
# Insights/content sync (graph.facebook.com, Facebook Login for
# Business). It is NOT used for messaging — see
# INSTAGRAM_MESSAGING_ACCESS_TOKEN below, a separate token for a
# separate Meta product. Mixing the two is what causes Graph API error
# "(#3) Application does not have the capability to make this API call."
INSTAGRAM_ACCESS_TOKEN=
INSTAGRAM_BUSINESS_ACCOUNT_ID=

# Instagram DM automation (src/app/api/webhooks/instagram/,
# src/app/api/cron/instagram-followups/) — comment/DM/story-reply
# keyword auto-replies with optional drip follow-ups. Outbound sends go
# through graph.instagram.com (the Instagram API with Instagram Login),
# a different product from the sync token above:
# - INSTAGRAM_MESSAGING_ACCESS_TOKEN = the Instagram Login token used to
#   BOOTSTRAP Auto-DM/messaging the first time only. See
#   src/lib/instagram/tokenStore.ts: on first use this seeds the
#   encrypted, auto-refreshing token stored in
#   instagram_integration_credentials (migration 0023) — after that,
#   outbound sends read from the database, not this env var, and
#   /api/cron/instagram-token-refresh keeps it alive automatically so it
#   never has to be manually regenerated every ~60 days. Also used by the
#   "Reconnect Instagram" admin action if the stored token ever becomes
#   unrecoverable. Generate it via the Meta App Dashboard → your
#   Instagram product → "API setup with Instagram login" with these
#   permissions: instagram_business_basic, instagram_business_manage_comments,
#   instagram_business_manage_messages. Do not reuse INSTAGRAM_ACCESS_TOKEN
#   here — it is the wrong token type for this endpoint.
# - INSTAGRAM_TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY = a base64-encoded 32-byte key used to
#   encrypt the stored messaging token at rest (AES-256-GCM — see
#   src/lib/instagram/tokenCrypto.ts). Generate one with
#   `openssl rand -base64 32`. Required before the token store can seed
#   itself — without it, messaging keeps working directly off
#   INSTAGRAM_MESSAGING_ACCESS_TOKEN (see tokenStore.ts's fallback), it
#   just won't be auto-refreshed.
# - INSTAGRAM_APP_SECRET: your Meta app's secret (App Dashboard →
#   Settings → Basic) — verifies that webhook POSTs really came from
#   Meta before any automated reply is sent, and is also required for the
#   token exchange call (ig_exchange_token) the token store uses.
# - INSTAGRAM_WEBHOOK_VERIFY_TOKEN: any string you make up yourself,
#   entered in the Meta app's webhook subscription setup — used once,
#   during Meta's verification handshake for the webhook URL.
# - CRON_SECRET: any string you make up yourself, used as the bearer
#   token an external scheduler (e.g. cron-job.org) sends when it pings
#   /api/cron/instagram-followups (due follow-up steps) and
#   /api/cron/instagram-token-refresh (daily token refresh check).
# See docs/INSTAGRAM_AUTOMATIONS_SETUP.md for full setup.
INSTAGRAM_MESSAGING_ACCESS_TOKEN=
INSTAGRAM_TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY=
INSTAGRAM_APP_SECRET=
INSTAGRAM_WEBHOOK_VERIFY_TOKEN=
CRON_SECRET=

# N4N DM Automations — a SEPARATE, dedicated Meta Developer App for
# Instagram messaging/automation only (comments + messages webhooks,
# outbound DM sends), additive alongside the existing Direct Instagram
# Login app above. See src/lib/instagram/instagramDmOAuth.ts,
# src/app/api/admin/social/instagram/connect-dm/{start,callback}, and
# src/app/api/webhooks/instagram/dm. Never reuses INSTAGRAM_APP_ID/SECRET
# or INSTAGRAM_LOGIN_APP_ID/SECRET — a missing value here fails loudly
# rather than silently falling back to either of those.
# - INSTAGRAM_DM_APP_ID / INSTAGRAM_DM_APP_SECRET = the N4N DM
#   Automations app's own Instagram app ID/secret, from that Meta App's
#   Dashboard → Instagram → API setup with Instagram Login → Business
#   login settings. Redirect URI to register there:
#   <your-site>/api/admin/social/instagram/connect-dm/callback
# - INSTAGRAM_DM_WEBHOOK_VERIFY_TOKEN = any string you make up yourself,
#   entered in the N4N DM Automations app's own webhook subscription
#   setup — used once, during Meta's verification handshake for
#   <your-site>/api/webhooks/instagram/dm. NOT the app secret, and NOT
#   the existing INSTAGRAM_WEBHOOK_VERIFY_TOKEN above (that one stays
#   for the existing /api/webhooks/instagram route only).
INSTAGRAM_DM_APP_ID=
INSTAGRAM_DM_APP_SECRET=
INSTAGRAM_DM_WEBHOOK_VERIFY_TOKEN=

# One-time bootstrap for the very first admin owner — see
# docs/ADMIN_AUTH_SETUP.md. Set this to the email of the person who
# should become owner, sign in with that email (Google or magic link) at
# /admin/login once, confirm they now have owner access, then remove
# this variable. Leaving it set is not a standing security hole (it only
# ever promotes this exact email, and only while no admin_users row
# exists yet for that account), but removing it once you're done is
# still good practice.
ADMIN_BOOTSTRAP_OWNER_EMAIL=

# ── LEGACY / REMOVE ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Replaced by Supabase Auth + admin_users (see docs/ADMIN_AUTH_SETUP.md).
# No code reads these anymore — safe to delete from your deployment once
# you've confirmed the new login works.
# ADMIN_PASSWORD=
# ADMIN_PASSWORD_TEMP=
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ADMIN_EMAIL=
